{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 720,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank You To Radiacode for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3680,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe is expanding and that expansion is \naccelerating under the power of dark energy and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8040,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eventually all matter and energy will be \ndispersed over such unthinkable distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that nothing can stop space from blowing up \ninfinitely. Unless of course cosmologists  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18280,
    "dDurationMs": 11200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "blundered and dark energy doesn't even \nexist. Then it's back to the drawing board."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29480,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone, quick update before \nwe start! We've observed that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "YouTube algorithm isn’t promoting our \nepisodes within the community as much,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we’re conducting an experiment. Over \nthe next few episodes, we’ll test whether  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44200,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing likes and comments impacts sharing. If \nyou'd like to be a data point in this experiment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hit like and/or leave a comment. \nAnd if you’re new and enjoying this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56360,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe and please press the bell. We’ll analyze \nthe results and report back after a few episodes!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63200,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A paper came out in December claiming \nthat dark energy—widely held to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66840,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dominant form of energy in the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68560,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and one the the central components of our \nmodern cosmological model—simply doesn’t exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The paper, by Antonia Seifert and team at \nthe University of Canterbury in New Zealand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80520,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "claims that the apparent accelerating expansion \ncomes from an oversimplification of our model of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expanding universe. The researchers state that \nthe current Lambda-CDM model doesn’t properly take  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into account the fact that time flows differently \nin different parts of the universe. In particular,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time flows slower in regions of strong gravity—so \nin the dense clusters and filaments of the cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104280,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "web. And it flows faster in the vast, empty \nregions in between. This results in a landscape  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111720,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of shifting time flow—in which some parts of \nthe universe may be billions of years older  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116960,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than others. So, instead of a landscape, more of a \ntimescape. The timescape model was first proposed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by David Wiltshire in 2007, and in this picture, \nfaster-ticking parts of the universe will have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131880,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expanded more than slower-time parts, and this \neffect can exactly mimic the apparent accelerating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion, but without any acceleration \nat all at least according to this team."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, people have been saying that dark energy \ndoesn’t exist since its discovery, with varying  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150160,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels of quackery. This particular proposal \ngot more attention than usual, in part because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156680,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s definitely not quackery. The proposal is \nscientifically well-founded and the researchers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 161120,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are serious people. There’s also a certain \nelegance to it that I hope I can convey to you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "None of this means the idea is right, and I’ll \nalso try to convey why it’s probably not true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173320,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First the super-quick review on dark \nenergy. The universe is expanding,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178240,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we’ve measured the history of that \nexpansion. One of the most important ways we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182280,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do that is by measuring the distances to Type 1-a \nsupernovae. These exploding white dwarf stars pop  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188840,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with predictable brightnesses. Light from these \nexplosions travels to us over great distances,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and two things happen on that journey. One is \nthat the light is dimmed as photons spread out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through the universe. This tells us how far and \nfor how much time the light traveled. Two is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those same photons are stretched—redshifted—by \nthe expansion of the universe as they travel.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211400,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This tells us how much expansion happened during \ntheir travel time. Put these together for many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216280,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernovae back through cosmic time and we \nget an expansion history for the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221360,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The results of the first big supernova surveys \ncame in in the late 90s, and scientists had  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226920,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expected to find that the expansion rate was \nslowing down due to gravity. It had to be because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s general theory of relativity only \nallowed an inward-pulling gravitational force.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To everyone’s great surprise, these studies \nfound that the expansion was accelerating.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244720,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first glance this made no sense under the \nthen-current version of general relativity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 249320,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, adding an additional piece to GR—the \ncosmological constant—provided exactly the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255320,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acceleration needed to explain the observations. \nWe don’t really know what causes this accelerating  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion—top contender is some sort of energy \nof the vacuum itself. But whatever it is, we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266680,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call it dark energy and its discovery scored our \nsupernova collectors the Nobel prize in physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274080,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The existence of dark energy in the form of a \ncosmological constant is now part of our dominant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280160,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mathematical description of how the universe \nworks on the largest scales— the Lambda-CDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287360,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lambda-CDM does such a good job of explaining \na whole range of observations that it’s hard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to avoid the conclusion that something like \ndark energy must exist. On the other hand,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve never really measured cosmic \nacceleration directly. We’ve just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301800,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found that models with dark energy fit the \ndata better than models without it. But what  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307440,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if there’s some other founding assumption \nbehind our model of cosmic expansion that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is wrong? That’s basically what \nthe timescape model is saying."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318200,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand the different factors driving the \nexpansion of the universe, we need to solve the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equations of general relativity for the entire \nuniverse. That’s only possible with some pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327720,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "major simplifications. The main one is that we \nassume that the matter in the universe is spread  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out smoothly everywhere—it’s both homogeneous and \nisotropic—no large-scale lumps or directionality.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That lets us describe the effect of matter \nwith a single number—the matter density—rather  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345920,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than having to solve for every chunk of matter \nseparately. The result is the Friedmann equations,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which we’ve picked apart before, and they describe \nhow the size of the universe changes over time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 355880,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "driven by smoothly distributed matter and \nconstant dark energy. Add the appropriate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361320,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amounts of both and you can describe our \nuniverse—and that’s the Lambda-CDM model."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367000,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the approximation of smoothness \nseems to be very good on large scales,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we know that the universe is not smooth on \nsmaller scales. At distances smaller than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a billion light years or so, this smooth matter \nhas fragmented into enormous galaxy clusters and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384840,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the filaments of matter connecting them. And bound \nby this cosmic web are giant empty patches—cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392120,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voids. These structures do have an effect on \nthe photons passing through them. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397880,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons gain some energy falling into deep \ngravitational fields, resulting in blue-shift,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and lose a little energy climbing out of \nthem, resulting in redshift. So a supernova  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that explodes somewhere deep in the Laniakea \nsupercluster might gain some redshift from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413000,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveling to us through the expanding space, and \nmore redshift from the fact that it has to fight  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418080,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against the supercluster’s gravitational field to \nreach the Milky Way, out on Laniakea’s periphery."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Local gravitational fields also affect the \nspeeds of nearby matter, and so while that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova is mostly moving away due to cosmic \nexpansion, it might have some extra velocity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "either towards or away due to its home galaxy \nmoving within Laniakea’s gravitational field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440000,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to take care to account for this stuff,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442800,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particularly for supernovae in the \nrelatively nearby universe. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we talk about the possible effect of \nlocal structure in a previous video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451600,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if the supernova happened more than a few \nhundred million light years away then its photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457040,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will pass through multiple voids and superclusters \non their way to us. All that blue-shifting and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463400,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "redshifting will cancel out. If that’s the case, \nthen the Friedmann equations and Lambda-CDM  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469200,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should do a perfectly good job at describing the \nexpanding universe experienced for those photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least, that’s what most people think. The \ntimescape model says otherwise. Like I said  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482040,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the start of this video, time flows slower \nin regions of high gravity and faster in low  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485760,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity—this gravitational time dilation is \na known and well tested feature of general  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity. But the timescape model proposed \nthat differences in time flow should have another  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497800,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect—it should change the rate of expansion \nof the universe from one place to another. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504400,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "straightforward enough. The more time that passes \nthe bigger the universe gets, so if more time has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510440,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "passed in a cosmic void compared to a region of \nhigher density, that void will have expanded more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This differential expansion has an effect on \nphotons traveling through these regions. For  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522400,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "example, photons are stretched out or redshifted \nmore as they travel through the faster-expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 527880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "voids compared to dense regions. As matter \nbecomes more and more clumped together over time,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 533480,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fraction of the universe with voids grows. \nThis means that photons traveling through the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539440,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "late universe spend more time in voids and \nso pick up more of this extra redshift."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544880,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Lambda-CDM model, it’s the accelerating \nexpansion of the universe that gives photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550800,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their extra redshift in later times. \nBut in the timescape model, it’s the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556600,
    "dDurationMs": 9920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increasing void fraction, and, supposedly, no \nglobal acceleration is needed in timescape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566520,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The timescape model has been \naround since 2007 and so "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570680,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the reason for the recent buzz is that the team \njust published their analysis of the results of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577400,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the biggest current supernova survey—Pantheon+. \nIn general they find that timescape gives a better  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 584480,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fit to the supernova data than Lambda-CDM. You \nca n see it on this plot, which shows the Bayes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "factor—basically the ratio of how good timescape \nis compared to Lambda-CDM. If we’re in the blue  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region then timescape is preferred. The x-axis \nis the redshift cut. Points on the left use all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the supernovae, including the nearby ones. As \nwe go to right we cut out supernovae starting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the closest to the more distant, so that over \nhere we’re only including the most distant ones.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617240,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a nice way to show the result because \nthe nearby supernovae should be more affected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622840,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by inhomogeneities—more influenced by the local \nlumpiness of the universe. So it makes some sense  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629840,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that Lambda-CDM doesn’t do as well there, with its \nassumption of perfect smoothness. Further away,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that lumpiness averages out and timescape \nand Lambda-CDM provide similar results,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642440,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "although according to this, timescape \nstill does a bit better at all distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648600,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, is this right? Has dark energy been \ndisproved? Well, if the only evidence for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653840,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy were these supernova results, \nthen this might be bad news for dark energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658720,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the lambda-CDM model. That’s because, in \nsome sense, the timescape model is simpler  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665280,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than Lambda-CDM. It only uses raw general \nrelativity, just as Einstein conceived it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671960,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with no need for the addition of a cosmological \nconstant. It uses the base theory plus the effect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678080,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of structures that we know exist in the universe. \nOccam’s razor tells us that if two hypotheses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yield identical results you should favor the \nsimpler hypothesis. By that logic timescape  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689720,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to win. The amount of time that \nlambda-CDM has been around should not,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695800,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by itself, make us favor the older theory. After \nall, Ptolemy’s Earth-centered model of the solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702920,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system was around for a millennium and a half \nbefore Copernicus came along with his simpler  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707680,
    "dDurationMs": 9640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun-centered model. But the test of time is only \nrelevant if it actually involves additional tests."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717320,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And indeed there are additional tests \nfor Lambda-CDM. This model provides a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good description of more than just \nthe supernova results. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729000,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "baryon acoustic oscillations—reverberating sound \nwaves from very early times—are now frozen as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "faint rings in the distribution of galaxies on the \nsky. The size of these rings gives an independent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741160,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "way to measure the expansion rate that is not \naffected by how cosmic voids redshift photons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747800,
    "dDurationMs": 8240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the BAO observations also strongly indicate \naccelerating expansion and support Lambda-CDM.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also the evolution of large scale \nstructure, which nicely match the simulations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761480,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we run under Lambda-CDM. And there’s the fact \nthat the universe is geometrically very flat,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768320,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and for that to be the case we need 70% \nof the energy in the universe to be dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772360,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy. It may be that the timescape model \ncan explain these too, but so far it hasn’t."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another major issue with the timescape model \nis that it requires quite large differences  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in timeflow between the cosmic voids and the \ndense regions of the universe—large enough that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790040,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billions of years more would have passed in the \nvoids compared to the superclusters. Such large  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796160,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "differences are not the consensus. For example, \nEthan Seigel points out that the relative flow  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time between these regions should result in an \nage differential of only 100s to 1000s of years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which isn’t anywhere near enough to produce \nthe effect that timescape claims. I should also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "point out that the researchers themselves admit \nthat more work needs to be done. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 819400,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova distances are not at all easy \nto figure out, and require many steps of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calibration. The timescape team acknowledges \nthat some of this requires further work."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829800,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, yeah, dark energy didn’t go away. Lambda-CDM \nstill gives us the most consistent description  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835960,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the large-scale universe, across \nmultiple independent observations.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840160,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, Lambda-CDM has its \nchallenges—there’s the now-famous Hubble  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844920,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tension—a mismatch between the expansion rate \nmeasured from supernovae versus the rate inferred  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 851040,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "under Lambda-CDM from observations of the cosmic \nmicrowave background. And then there’s the recent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856480,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark Energy Survey Instrument result that the \ncosmological constant may be actually changing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861000,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time. It’s not clear that timescape can \nsort out these issues, but any chink in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "armour of Lambda-CDM is a good motivation for \nexploring other models. And maybe part of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872400,
    "dDurationMs": 8320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solution will for us to be more careful in how \nwe model the temporal landscape of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880720,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to radiacode for supporting PBS. If \nyou’re looking for a new science based hobby,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Radiacode is a gadget that’s made for all \nnatural science enthusiasts. Radiacode is a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889720,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "portable radiation detector that collects data \nfast. Now you can not only quickly locate the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893800,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "source of radiation but also identify which \nisotopes are present, such as Cesium-137,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898320,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thorium-232, Radium-226, and many others. The \nmultifunctional app, included with the device,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903800,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "works on iOS or Android and makes it easier \nto explore and research by allowing you to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908080,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "record radioactive GPS tracks on maps, keep a \nmeasurement log, analyze spectra, create charts,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and build spectrograms. You can learn more about \nRadiaCode by following the link in the description  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917000,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the pinned comment below. Use the promo \ncode \"PBS\" to get an exclusive 10% discount."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Before we go we wanted to let you \nknow, we’re celebrating the 50th anniversary of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 928240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Space Shuttle patent, with some limited \nedition merch at the merch store. There’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933440,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "multicolored hoodies and t-shirts featuring \nthe original patent, the first ever space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time notebook as well as a re-release of the \nmutli-functional space time pen in new colors.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944320,
    "dDurationMs": 14600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can check it out at pbsspacetime.com/shop. \nThere’s a link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
